  al. v. LOPEZ\, ATTORNEY GENERAL \nOF HAWAII \ncertiorari to the united st
 ates court of appeals for \nthe ninth circuit \nNo. 24–1046. Argued Janu
 ary 20\, 2026—Decided June 25\, 2026 \nFor years\, the State of Hawaii m
 ade it almost impossible to obtain a li-\ncense to carry a frearm. Four ye
 ars ago\, however\, this Court held in \nNew York State Rife & Pistol Assn
 .\, Inc. v. Bruen\, 597 U. S. 1\, that the \nSecond and Fourteenth Amendme
 nts protect the right to carry hand-\nguns outside the home for self-defen
 se. Hawaii responded by replacing \nits old law on carry permits with new 
 laws that achieved a similar result. \nAt issue in this case is a Hawaii l
 aw that prohibits frearms on private \nproperty open to the public without
  the express and affrmative consent \nof the property owner. Hawaii's new 
 rule imposes severe restrictions \non the daily activities of residents wh
 o have satisfed the State's rigor-\nous requirements for the issuance of a
  carry permit. When these per-\nmit holders leave home\, not only must the
 y take care to avoid all the \nterritory where the possession of a gun is 
 prohibited outright\, but they \nmay also be barred from entering many pla
 ces that people routinely \nvisit in the course of their daily routines\, 
 such as gas stations\, restau-\nrants\, and stores. This law fips the defa
 ult rule at common law\, under \nwhich anyone has an implied license to en
 ter property held open to the \npublic unless the property owner withdraws
  consen\nhttps://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/25pdf/609us1r58_a8cf.pdf
URL:https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/25pdf/609us1r58_a8cf.pdf
CATEGORIES:Federal Maine-Related
